Major changes with snapshot 20260502
====================================

New "smtp_allow_plaintext_session" and "smtpd_allow_plaintext_session"
parameters that, when set to "no", refuse to send or accept mail over
an unencrypted SMTP channel regardless of *_tls_security_level. The
flag is layered on top of the existing security level: when "no", any
sub-ENCRYPT level resolved by the policy machinery (NONE, MAY, or a
sender's "TLS-Required: no" downgrade) is promoted to ENCRYPT, the
remote peer must offer STARTTLS, and a failed handshake defers the
message instead of falling back to cleartext.

A "yes" setting preserves Postfix 3.12 behavior. The default is
compatibility-level gated: with "compatibility_level >= 3.13" the
default flips to "no". This matches the precedent used for
smtpd_relay_before_recipient_restrictions and smtp_tls_security_level.

The recommended deployment is to enable the veto per-listener (in
master.cf for submission/submissions) and per-transport (for relay
to known-TLS smarthops), and keep the global default "yes" for any
MTA that delivers to arbitrary Internet MX hosts. Setting the flag
globally on such an MTA will defer mail to every recipient whose MX
does not offer STARTTLS, until the queue lifetime expires.

The Postfix SMTP server treats "smtpd_allow_plaintext_session = no"
as if "smtpd_tls_security_level = encrypt" and "smtpd_tls_auth_only = yes"
were set, except when invoked via "sendmail -bs" (which has no access
to the server private key); in the latter case the flag is ignored,
matching the existing local-IPC fallback behavior.

A sender-supplied "TLS-Required: no" header (RFC 8689) cannot bypass
the operator's "smtp_allow_plaintext_session = no" setting on the
SMTP client. The header would normally downgrade the security level
to MAY; with the veto in effect, the policy resolver promotes that
downgrade to ENCRYPT.

Incompatible changes with snapshot 20260312
===========================================

For consistency with postmap, the postalias command now stores the
alias database search key in external (quoted) form. For example,
if an alias name contains whitespace between the words 'foo' and
'bar', then it will be stored as "foo bar" including the double
quotes.

For consistency with "postmap -s", the "postalias -s" command now
outputs the search key in external (quoted) form.

For backwards compatibility, the local(8) delivery agent will look
up the legacy internal (unquoted) form of an alias name only if the
preferred external (quoted) form is not found, and the internal and
external forms differ.
